course: dictionary
lesson: 13545
title: Not exact: the word imprecise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you how exact something is. Imprecise means not exact or accurate. Its opposite is precise, which means exact. The prefix im- often means not, so imprecise is the opposite of precise.
explain: «Sam measured the desk with his hand span, so his answer was imprecise.» A hand span is rough, not a ruler. Sam's answer is close, but it is not exact. Ask yourself: is this exact, or is it a rough guess?
explain: Imprecise can also describe words and instructions. «The directions were imprecise: go past the big tree, somewhere near the river.» Where exactly is the river? The reader cannot tell, so the instructions are vague and unclear.
fact: Imprecise means not exact or accurate; rough or vague.
fact: The opposite of imprecise is precise, which means exact.
fact: «Imprecise» is an adjective. The noun is «imprecision».
fact: «An imprecise measurement» is not exact enough, like measuring a pencil with steps instead of a ruler.
fact: «Imprecise instructions» are not clearly expressed, so the reader may get confused.
exercise: «Sam measured the table with his hand span, so his answer was imprecise.» What does imprecise mean here? | Painted in bright colours | *Not exact | Very long | Measured with a ruler
exercise: «The directions were imprecise: go past the big tree, somewhere near the river.» Which words show the directions are not exact? | *somewhere near the river | The river | Go past the big tree | The tree
exercise: Mia says imprecise means the same as precise. Why is she wrong? | Both words mean exact | *Precise means exact, and imprecise means not exact | Precise means rough or vague | Imprecise means very fast
test: «The scientist wrote an imprecise measurement, so she measured it again with a ruler.» Why did she measure it again? | To make the measurement longer | To make it louder | *To make the measurement more exact | To change its colour
test: Which word is the noun form of imprecise? | imprecisely | *imprecision | precisely | precise
exam: Which phrase best describes a guess about how many sweets are in a jar? | a precise ruler | an exact count | a perfect measurement | *an imprecise estimate
